Intents with distinctive vocabulary (greetings, events, gacha) work well. The weak ones overlap
with each other. The confusion matrix is in `chatbot_backend/confusion_matrix.png`.

Where the classifier goes wrong (22 errors out of 62)

1. game_info mistaken for player_review (5 of the 9 game_info questions).
   Examples: "what company developed fgo", "what platform is fgo on", "what languages is fgo
   available in". These are factual questions, but they contain the same words ("fgo", "game") as
   opinion questions such as "is fgo worth playing". With about 40 examples per intent, the model
   cannot reliably tell the two apart.
2. servant_info mistaken for fallback (3 of 7).
   Examples: "who is scathach", "who is altria", "who is merlin". A name question gives the model
   very little to work with: the name is a word it has rarely seen, so its confidence is only 0.18,
   below the 0.20 cutoff. *In the full chatbot these questions are answered correctly anyway*,
   because the servant-name recognition step finds the name regardless of the classifier's confidence.
3. Short or vague questions get low confidence.
   Examples: "what is command seal" (0.18), "do people still play this" (0.18), "when is the next
   anniversary" (0.17). Few words means little for TF-IDF to use.
4. Off-topic questions that share words with the domain.
   "have you played other gacha games" was read as player_review (shares "gacha", "games"), and
   "tell me a joke" as servant_info (the word "tell" appears in many servant questions, such as
   "tell me about gilgamesh").

The pattern: most errors happen between intents that share vocabulary, or on short questions
where the model has little evidence.

What was tried to improve it

| Change | Accuracy / macro F1 | Result |
| Baseline, 170 questions | 0.59 / 0.61 | starting point |
| Add word pairs (bigrams) | 0.59 / 0.61 | no change: too little data for two-word phrases to repeat |
| Add 13 questions to one intent only (183 rows) | 0.46 / 0.44 | worse: the model leaned toward the intent with more data (class imbalance) |
| Same, with `class_weight='balanced'` | 0.51 / 0.51 | partly corrected, still below baseline |
| Expand to 294 balanced questions | 0.61 / 0.62 | small gain from more data |
| Add 8 targeted game_info questions (302) | 0.67 / 0.67 | game_info recall 0.29 to 0.89, but player_review dropped (F1 0.67 to 0.44) |
| Add 8 targeted player_review questions (310) | 0.65 / 0.66 | player_review recovered, but game_info dropped again |

Fixing one of the two overlapping intents costs the other. This is a limit of the data size
(about 40 questions per intent), not something more tuning would fix, so the final model is
the 310-question version.

How the full chatbot makes up for the classifier's weaknesses

- Confidence cutoff (0.20).mBelow it the bot says it did not understand instead of guessing.
  Lowering it from 0.25 to 0.20 gave 7 more correct answers for 3 more wrong ones on the test data.
- Servant-name recognition. Finds a servant's name in the message (handling accents, hyphens,
  last names, nicknames and small typos), so name questions work even when the classifier is unsure.
- Class questions. "saber" or "what are the servant classes" get a real answer with the class
  matchups and servant counts taken from the game data.
- Two kinds of fallback. An off-topic question gets a polite "not my topic"; an unclear one
  gets "could you rephrase?".

The 5 questions the full chatbot still gets wrong: "heya" (a word it never saw), "give me a quick
overview of this game", "is there any guaranteed pull" (confidence 0.19, just under the cutoff),
"is anything happening in the game right now", and "tell me a joke".

Other errors found and fixed (not classifier errors)

Testing the replies, not just the classifier, found mistakes in the written answers:
- A reply said three cards of one type make a "Brave Chain". Wrong: that needs three cards from
  the same servant. Fixed.
- Replies said the game has "no pity system". Wrong for the English server, which guarantees the
  featured servant by the 330th summon on limited banners (checked against official pages). Fixed.
- A servant's "notable traits" always listed a trait nearly every servant has. Removed.
- The first message after starting the server took 1.2 seconds because a language dictionary loaded
  lazily. A startup warm-up brought it down to 22 ms.

Accuracy numbers do not catch this kind of error, so the replies were checked separately.

Limitations

- The 310 questions were written by one person, not collected from real users. Real users may
  phrase things differently, so real-world accuracy could be lower than reported.
- The test set is small (62 questions), so one question changes accuracy by 1.6 points.
  Cross-validation (0.58 to 0.79 across folds) shows how much the result can vary.
- Craft essences and materials are not covered. Questions about them get an unrelated reply.
- Answers about the gacha and events are general, because the game changes often.

How it could be improved

- Collect real questions from users and add many more examples per intent, especially for
  game_info versus player_review and for servant names.
- Try sentence embeddings (semantic similarity), which compare meaning instead of shared words and
  should separate the overlapping intents better.
